{
  "title": "Evidence-Backed Food Security & Nutrition Interventions Synthesis",
  "content": "This plan decomposes the comprehensive research goal into parallel sub-agent tasks, each responsible for deeply researching 2 intervention contexts. Each sub-agent will gather evidence on specific interventions, their cost-effectiveness data, implementation requirements, and known failure modes from authoritative sources (World Bank, J-PAL, 3ie, IFPRI, Lancet, Copenhagen Consensus, etc.). A final reasoning task synthesizes all findings into a unified structured summary. The prior search context has identified key source URLs that sub-agents can leverage.",
  "tasks": [
    {
      "goal": "Research and summarize evidence-backed interventions for EMERGENCY RESPONSE (cash vs in-kind food aid, therapeutic feeding programs like CMAM/RUTF, emergency school feeding, rapid response mechanisms) and SOCIAL PROTECTION (conditional/unconditional cash transfers, public works programs like PSNP, school feeding, food vouchers). For each intervention: identify cost-effectiveness metrics (cost per DALY, cost per beneficiary, benefit-cost ratios), implementation requirements (institutional capacity, targeting mechanisms, supply chains, M&E systems), and known failure modes (targeting errors, dependency effects, market distortion, political capture, elite capture, delayed response). Use sources like WFP evaluation summaries, J-PAL/3ie evidence reviews, World Bank social protection reports, and Lancet nutrition series.",
      "tool_name": "spawn_sub_agent",
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "goal": "Research and summarize evidence-backed interventions for NUTRITION (micronutrient supplementation like vitamin A/zinc/iron-folic acid, IYCF counseling, nutrition-sensitive agriculture, community-based nutrition programs, ready-to-use therapeutic foods) and FORTIFICATION/BIOFORTIFICATION (large-scale food fortification of staples like flour/oil/salt, biofortified crops like orange-fleshed sweet potato/iron beans/zinc wheat/provitamin A maize, point-of-use fortification with MNP sachets). For each intervention: identify cost-effectiveness metrics (cost per DALY averted, Copenhagen Consensus BCRs, cost per child reached), implementation requirements (regulatory frameworks, food vehicle selection, supply chain, quality assurance, consumer acceptance), and known failure modes (low compliance, poor coverage, inadequate monitoring, bioavailability challenges, consumer rejection, industrial capacity gaps). Use sources like Lancet nutrition series, HarvestPlus evidence reports, Copenhagen Consensus, WHO e-Library of Evidence for Nutrition Actions (eLENA), and CGIAR/IFPRI publications.",
      "tool_name": "spawn_sub_agent",
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "goal": "Research and summarize evidence-backed interventions for SMALLHOLDER PRODUCTIVITY (improved seed varieties, fertilizer subsidies/smart subsidies, extension services including digital/ICT-based, irrigation and water management, integrated soil fertility management, mechanization) and MARKET ACCESS (warehouse receipt systems, contract farming, farmer cooperatives/producer organizations, market information systems/digital platforms, rural roads and infrastructure, value chain development). For each intervention: identify cost-effectiveness metrics (yield gains, income effects, benefit-cost ratios from RCTs and quasi-experimental studies), implementation requirements (input supply chains, institutional support, land tenure, financial access, aggregation infrastructure), and known failure modes (elite capture of subsidies, low adoption rates, technology mismatch with local conditions, market power asymmetries, cooperative governance failures, displacement effects). Use sources like J-PAL agricultural policy insights, 3ie systematic reviews, IFPRI research, World Bank agricultural investment sourcebooks, and IDB/FAO evaluations.",
      "tool_name": "spawn_sub_agent",
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "goal": "Research and summarize evidence-backed interventions for POST-HARVEST LOSS REDUCTION (hermetic storage bags like PICS/GrainPro, improved granaries, cold chain infrastructure, solar drying, metal silos, aggregation and processing centers) and RESILIENCE/INSURANCE (index-based weather insurance, area-yield insurance, savings groups, climate-smart agriculture practices, early warning systems, social insurance mechanisms, forecast-based financing). For each intervention: identify cost-effectiveness metrics (% loss reduction, ROI, cost per farmer protected, actuarial sustainability), implementation requirements (farmer training, last-mile distribution, basis risk management, reinsurance, weather station density, mobile money infrastructure, behavioral nudges for uptake), and known failure modes (basis risk in index insurance, low demand/willingness to pay, adverse selection, storage technology adoption barriers, cold chain maintenance failures, moral hazard, high transaction costs for small premiums). Use sources like Rockefeller Foundation YieldWise reports, APHLIS data, UC Davis BASIS program on index insurance, J-PAL insurance policy insights, IFPRI climate-smart agriculture evidence, and IRI/Columbia climate services research.",
      "tool_name": "spawn_sub_agent",
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "goal": "Synthesize all findings from the four research streams into a comprehensive, structured summary organized by the 8 intervention contexts. For each context, produce a standardized entry with: (1) Top 3-5 evidence-backed interventions ranked by strength of evidence and cost-effectiveness, (2) Cost-effectiveness comparison table with metrics standardized where possible, (3) Implementation requirements checklist, (4) Known failure modes and mitigation strategies, (5) Cross-cutting insights on complementarities between intervention types (e.g., social protection + nutrition, market access + post-harvest loss reduction). Include a final section on portfolio-level recommendations for combining interventions across contexts for maximum impact.",
      "tool_name": "reasoning",
      "depends_on": [0, 1, 2, 3]
    }
  ],
  "dependencies_graph": [
    {"task_idx": "0", "depends_on": []},
    {"task_idx": "1", "depends_on": []},
    {"task_idx": "2", "depends_on": []},
    {"task_idx": "3", "depends_on": []},
    {"task_idx": "4", "depends_on": ["0", "1", "2", "3"]}
  ],
  "join": "all"
}
